{
  "result": "On the first warm day in March, someone hung a small red mitten on the fence outside the laundromat. It looked like a hand raised to ask a question. By noon the snow beneath it had melted, exposing a penny, two cigarette filters, and a green plastic dinosaur with no head.\n\nI went inside to wash my sheets. A man was folding towels with such care that I found myself slowing down to watch. He lined up the corners, ran the edge of his hand along each fold, and stacked the towels in a blue grocery bag. When he caught me looking, he said, \u201cMy daughter likes them this way.\u201d Then he shook out another towel.\n\nI had come with a book I meant to finish, but I spent most of the wash cycle watching people walk past the window. Three stopped at the fence. A woman touched the mitten and kept going. A boy put it on, showed his friend, and put it back. The third person was the man with the towels. He moved it to a higher prong, where you could see it from the bus stop.\n\nBy evening the mitten was gone. I know this because I went back for the book, which I had left on top of a dryer. Someone had tucked a clean dryer sheet between the pages to hold my place. It wasn\u2019t my place, but I started there anyway. The first sentence on the page was one I didn\u2019t remember reading.",
